---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = "ITI Career Options: Which Trade to Choose and Where It Leads";
const seoTitle = "ITI Career Options: Trades, Apprenticeship, Govt Routes";
const description =
  "ITI career options after 8th or 10th: trades by sector, apprenticeship, railway and Agniveer routes, diploma lateral entry, and how to spot a fake ITI.";
const canonical = "https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/college-degrees/iti-career-options/";
const publishedDateISO = "2026-10-14";
const publishedDateLabel = "14 October 2026";
const authorName = "Shivanshi Sehgal";
const authorUrl = "https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/";
const authorRole = "Career researcher and writer";

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: "answer", label: "The short answer" },
  { id: "basics", label: "NCVT, SCVT and what the certificate is" },
  { id: "eligibility", label: "Eligibility after 8th, 10th and 12th" },
  { id: "trades", label: "Trades by sector" },
  { id: "scale", label: "Trades ranked by scalability" },
  { id: "check", label: "The Bench, Boss and Business Check" },
  { id: "fake", label: "Fake ITI affiliation: how to check" },
  { id: "apprentice", label: "Apprenticeship after ITI" },
  { id: "govt", label: "Government and PSU routes" },
  { id: "diploma", label: "ITI to diploma lateral entry" },
  { id: "vs", label: "ITI or polytechnic?" },
  { id: "business", label: "Four business layers" },
  { id: "ai", label: "EV, solar, AI and automation" },
  { id: "money", label: "Fees, scholarships and debt" },
  { id: "mistakes", label: "Expensive wrong turns" },
  { id: "next-step", label: "What to do next" },
  { id: "faq", label: "FAQs" },
];

const eligibilityRows = [
  { level: "Class 8 pass", opens: "A smaller set of trades. One government ITI lists Plumber and Sewing Technology as one-year trades for Class 8 pass.", watch: "Many popular trades ask for Class 10. In some states the same trade needs Class 8 under the state council (SCVT) and Class 10 with Maths and Science under the national council (NCVT)." },
  { level: "Class 10 pass", opens: "Most trades. COPA, Food Production and Food and Beverage Service Assistant list Class 10 pass.", watch: "Engineering trades such as Electrician, Fitter and Mechanic Refrigeration and Air Conditioning list Class 10 pass with Science and Maths." },
  { level: "Class 10 fail", opens: "Only the Class 8 trades, if you hold a Class 8 pass. Otherwise clear Class 10 first, through a supplementary attempt or open schooling.", watch: "Ask the ITI for its written eligibility list for your trade. Rules differ by state and by institute." },
  { level: "Class 12 (any stream, including commerce)", opens: "Every trade open to a Class 10 pass is open to you. The ITI checks your Class 10 subjects, not your Class 12 stream.", watch: "A commerce student who skipped Science and Maths in Class 10 may be shut out of engineering trades but can still take COPA and service trades. Some newer trades ask for Class 12, so read each notice." },
];

const tradeRows = [
  { sector: "Electrical and energy", trades: "Electrician, Wireman, Solar Technician (Electrical)", entry: "Electrician is listed as a two-year trade for Class 10 pass with Science and Maths. Solar Technician appears as a new trade in the 2026 affiliation orders and as a one-year course at a national skill institute.", hires: "Factories, utilities, contractors, builders, solar installers, households." },
  { sector: "Auto and electric vehicles", trades: "Mechanic Motor Vehicle, Mechanic Diesel, Mechanic Electric Vehicle", entry: "Diesel is listed as one year in one state for Class 10 with Maths and Science under NCVT and Class 8 under SCVT. One state ITI lists Mechanic Electric Vehicle as a two-year state-council trade.", hires: "Dealerships, service centres, fleet operators, local garages." },
  { sector: "Cooling and refrigeration", trades: "Mechanic Refrigeration and Air Conditioning", entry: "Two years, Class 10 with Science and Maths, in the Goa listing.", hires: "Service companies, malls, hotels, hospitals, home service." },
  { sector: "Fabrication and machine shop", trades: "Fitter, Turner, Machinist, Welder, Sheet Metal Worker", entry: "Fitter is two years for Class 10 with Science and Maths. Welder and Sheet Metal Worker fall in the one-year group in a PSU apprenticeship notice.", hires: "Factories, workshops, shipyards, construction and fabrication units." },
  { sector: "Electronics and instruments", trades: "Electronics Mechanic, Instrument Mechanic, Laboratory Assistant", entry: "Check the state prospectus. These trades appear in public sector apprenticeship notices at the higher stipend tier.", hires: "Electronics makers, plants, labs, repair and installation shops." },
  { sector: "Construction and plumbing", trades: "Plumber, Carpenter, Draughtsman (Mechanical)", entry: "Plumber is one year for Class 8 pass in the Goa listing. Carpenter is a one-year trade in a PSU notice.", hires: "Builders, contractors, housing societies, home owners." },
  { sector: "Computer and office", trades: "COPA (Computer Operator and Programming Assistant)", entry: "One year, Class 10 pass.", hires: "Offices, shops, schools and small firms. You compete with graduates for the same desk jobs." },
  { sector: "Food, hospitality and sewing", trades: "Food Production, Food and Beverage Service Assistant, Sewing Technology", entry: "One year each in the Goa listing. Food trades need Class 10, Sewing Technology needs Class 8.", hires: "Hotels, restaurants, caterers, garment units, boutiques." },
  { sector: "New-age technician trades", trades: "Semiconductor Technician, Industrial IoT, Green Hydrogen Production Technician, Cyber Security Assistant, AI Programming Assistant", entry: "A national skill institute lists these for Class 10 pass, from one year (most) to two years (Industrial IoT).", hires: "Few employers so far. Seats and local jobs are limited, so ask for the last batch's job list." },
];

const scaleRows = [
  { trade: "Electrician, Wireman and Solar Technician", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Wiring, panels and rooftop solar are local services that households and shops pay for directly. A salaried plant job follows a fixed ladder.", who: "Who pays: home owners, shops, builders. They trust a neighbour's referral and a photo log of finished jobs. First sales step: one clear price per common job, then ask each happy customer for one referral." },
  { trade: "Mechanic Electric Vehicle and Motor Vehicle", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Dealership pay follows a ladder. A garage or a two-wheeler and three-wheeler EV service bench is the business layer.", who: "Who pays: vehicle owners and small fleets. First sales step: a written estimate before work, and a repeat-service reminder list." },
  { trade: "Mechanic Refrigeration and Air Conditioning", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Cooling needs yearly service, so repeat customers are built in. Home and shop service contracts are the layer.", who: "Who pays: homes, shops, clinics. First sales step: a seasonal service offer at a fixed price, sold to the customers you already have." },
  { trade: "Plumber and Carpenter", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Demand is local and steady. Small contracting and fit-out work give the layer.", who: "Who pays: home owners and builders. First sales step: before and after photos and a fixed rate for repeat jobs." },
  { trade: "Fitter, Turner, Machinist, Welder, Sheet Metal Worker", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Plant jobs follow a fixed ladder. A small job-work shop, fabrication or maintenance contract is the layer, but it needs tools and working capital.", who: "Who pays: small factories and builders. First sales step: one documented job with measurements, then a short list of what you can make or fix." },
  { trade: "Electronics Mechanic and Instrument Mechanic", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Salaried plant roles are modest. Repair benches and installation of cameras, inverters and instruments can be sold directly.", who: "Who pays: shops, offices and small makers. First sales step: a fixed price list for the five repairs you do best." },
  { trade: "Food, hospitality and Sewing Technology", label: "Scalable with a business layer", cls: "tag-mid", why: "Kitchen and service jobs are shift-heavy with modest pay. Catering, a tiffin kitchen, tailoring or alteration work is the layer.", who: "Who pays: households, offices, boutiques. First sales step: a small repeat-order menu or alteration price list shared with ten known customers." },
  { trade: "COPA", label: "Not so scalable", cls: "tag-low", why: "Entry office roles are crowded and graduates also apply. It scales only when paired with a skill people pay for, such as accounting software, design or web basics.", who: "Who pays: the employer. Add a second skill and one finished sample before you rely on COPA alone." },
  { trade: "New-age technician trades", label: "Not so scalable", cls: "tag-low", why: "The employer base is small and we found no job-outcome data. Treat them as bets until a nearby employer confirms demand.", who: "Who pays: the employer, almost always. Ask the institute for the last batch's placed list before you pay." },
];

const benchChecks = [
  { num: "1", title: "Bench: can you do this work on a bad day?", body: "Spend a day with an electrician, mechanic, welder or plumber. Note heat, noise, heights, shifts and safety rules, and which tasks you would still enjoy in year three.", note: "Many students pick a trade by name. Pick it by the boring 80% of the day." },
  { num: "2", title: "Boss: who within reach will train and hire you?", body: "List the factories, service centres, contractors and PSU units within a few hours of home that take ITI apprentices. No nearby employer means moving at 17 or 18, or a weaker first job.", note: "Count real employers, not trade names on a brochure." },
  { num: "3", title: "Business: what could you sell by year three?", body: "A wiring job, a service visit, a repair, a stitched order. If the honest answer is nothing, pick a trade where something sells, or add a second skill.", note: "This question moves a trade from salary-only to scalable." },
];

const affiliationSteps = [
  { num: "01", title: "Look the ITI up on the official portal", body: "Search the NCVT MIS site (ncvtmis.gov.in) by state and district. An explainer from a skills non-profit says a private ITI that is not listed there has a false affiliation claim." },
  { num: "02", title: "Check the trade, not just the name", body: "Affiliation is given by trade and unit, and DGT orders list them one ITI at a time. An ITI can be affiliated for Electrician and not for Solar Technician. Ask which trade, how many units and for which session." },
  { num: "03", title: "Ask which certificate you will get", body: "National council trades give the National Trade Certificate. State council trades give the State Trade Certificate. Railway and many PSU notices ask for an ITI recognised by NCVT or SCVT, so check your target notice." },
  { num: "04", title: "Walk through the workshop", body: "Each trade needs its own workshop with equipment that shows signs of use. A DGT affiliation order also requires the ITI to show its logo on the main gate board and allows inspection at any time." },
  { num: "05", title: "Count heads and check instructors", body: "The number of students present should match the claimed intake. Instructors should hold the qualification the trade syllabus asks for. Ask for the last batch's placement list you can call and verify." },
  { num: "06", title: "Pay by traceable receipt", body: "Fees are set under state norms and private ITIs have no single national ceiling. Get the fee list in writing and pay only against a receipt. Complaints go to the state vocational training directorate, which can withdraw recognition." },
];

const govtRows = [
  { route: "Railway Group D (Level 1)", notice: "The CEN 09/2025 notice listed 22,195 posts, and applications closed on 9 March 2026. Education: Class 10 pass, or an NCVT or SCVT ITI, or a National Apprenticeship Certificate. Pay is Level 1 with basic pay of Rs 18,000 a month. Stages: computer test, physical test, document check, medical. Sources differ on the age limit (one says 18 to 36, another a base of 33), so read the current notice.", catch: "Many applicants compete for each post and the physical test is a second hard stage. Keep a trade or apprenticeship running while you prepare." },
  { route: "Agniveer (Army)", notice: "Recent notices give an age band of 17.5 to 22 years. The Technical category lists Class 12 science with Physics, Maths and English at 50%, or Class 12 plus a one-year ITI at NSQF level 4 or above. One older notice also listed Class 10 with a two-year ITI or diploma, and we could not confirm it is current.", catch: "Agniveer is a four-year engagement and only a share of each batch is kept on. ITI alone after Class 10 is not the main door, so check which category lists your trade on the official Army site." },
  { route: "PSU and defence unit apprenticeships (HAL, NFC and similar)", notice: "Recent notices ask for an ITI pass in the trade, NAPS registration and, at one unit, Class 10 plus ITI with a maximum age of 25 for General, 28 for OBC and 30 for SC and ST. No application fee was listed.", catch: "This is training under the Apprentices Act, not a job. One notice states that it does not guarantee regular employment." },
  { route: "State and other public posts", notice: "State electricity boards, municipal bodies and other departments list ITI trades for technician and helper posts, with rules set by each notice.", catch: "Cycles and trades vary. Treat any one exam as a bet." },
];

const businessLayers = [
  { label: "Layer 1", title: "Workshop or job-work shop", body: "A fitter, turner, machinist or welder who has worked in a factory can take small jobs from nearby units. It needs machines, space and working capital, so start by taking weekend jobs while employed.", fit: "Who pays: small factories and builders. They trust finished samples and on-time delivery.", watch: "Watch: a few big customers can leave overnight. Aim for several small ones." },
  { label: "Layer 2", title: "Contracting", body: "Wiring, plumbing, fit-out and fabrication jobs for housing societies and builders. Many states license electrical contractors, so check your state's rules before you take a contract.", fit: "Who pays: builders and societies. They trust referrals and a clean record of past sites.", watch: "Watch: late payment. Put scope, price and an advance in writing before you start." },
  { label: "Layer 3", title: "Repair and service", body: "Air conditioner, refrigerator, two-wheeler, inverter and motor repair is a recurring need. Yearly service gives repeat customers without new advertising.", fit: "Who pays: homes and shops. They trust the person who fixed the last fault properly.", watch: "Watch: parts cost and warranty disputes. Give a written estimate first." },
  { label: "Layer 4", title: "Solar and EV service", body: "Rooftop solar maintenance and small-vehicle EV servicing are newer service lines. The 2026 affiliation orders list Solar Technician as an affiliated trade at many ITIs.", fit: "Who pays: home owners, shops and small fleets.", watch: "Watch: a certificate is not demand. Ask local solar installers and EV dealers whether they hire ITI pass-outs before you choose these trades." },
];

const vsRows = [
  { q: "Entry", iti: "Class 8 or Class 10, depending on the trade", poly: "Class 10 pass; some states ask for Maths and set a test or merit list" },
  { q: "Length", iti: "Six months to two years in most trades", poly: "About three years" },
  { q: "Style", iti: "Mostly workshop practice with some theory", poly: "More theory and drawing alongside labs" },
  { q: "Typical first work", iti: "Technician, helper, apprentice, service work", poly: "Supervisor, junior engineer routes, technician" },
  { q: "Bridge upward", iti: "Second-year diploma entry in some places, then lateral entry to B.Tech", poly: "Lateral entry to B.Tech second year" },
];

const aiRows = [
  { label: "Now", title: "Electric vehicles and solar add work, not just risk", body: "Rooftop solar and electric two-wheelers need people who can install, test and repair them. Affiliated trade lists now name Solar Technician and Mechanic Electric Vehicle. No demand figure is promised, so check local listings." },
  { label: "Task level", title: "Routine tasks shrink, site work stays", body: "AI helps with fault lookup, estimates and manuals. It does not climb a pole, crimp a joint or sign off a wiring job. Presence and accountability stay human." },
  { label: "What to learn", title: "Add one digital layer beside the trade", body: "Read circuit and service manuals with an AI tool, then check the answer against your trainer. Learn a spreadsheet for estimates, basic PLC or automation ideas, and EV or inverter basics." },
  { label: "Later", title: "Automation and maintenance technician roles", body: "As plants add automated lines, people who can maintain them are needed. DGT and national skill institutes list technician courses in industrial IoT and semiconductor work. Treat them as bets until nearby employers confirm demand." },
];

const gateItems = [
  { name: "Gate 1", title: "Proof of skill", body: "Spend a day shadowing a tradesperson, or fix one small thing at home and write down what you did." },
  { name: "Gate 2", title: "Proof of communication", body: "Explain in a minute why this trade fits you and what you will sell by year three, in English or your own language." },
  { name: "Gate 3", title: "Proof of value", body: "Ask three working people what they earned in year one and year three, and which route they took after the ITI." },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  { num: "01", title: "Paying a private ITI before checking the portal", body: "A certificate from an institute with no valid affiliation can fail at a recruitment desk. Look it up by trade first." },
  { num: "02", title: "Choosing the trade with the shortest queue", body: "An easy seat in a trade with no nearby employer is a poor deal. Use the Bench, Boss and Business Check." },
  { num: "03", title: "Stopping at the certificate", body: "The certificate gets you in the door. A job log with photos, a reference and one extra skill gets you the offer." },
  { num: "04", title: "Taking a loan for an ITI", body: "A government ITI is cheap and the credential is not legally required for most trades. Borrowing for it is hard to justify." },
  { num: "05", title: "Betting everything on one exam", body: "Group D and Agniveer are multi-stage and crowded. Run an apprenticeship or a paid trade skill in parallel." },
];

const faqItems = [
  { q: "What are the career options after ITI?", a: "You can work as a technician or helper, join a one-year apprenticeship, apply for railway, defence and PSU posts that accept ITI, start a repair or contracting business, or continue to a diploma through second-year entry where your state allows it. The strongest outcomes usually combine an apprenticeship with one added skill and a small service business." },
  { q: "Is ITI a good career?", a: "It is a good start if you like hands-on work and want income sooner and cheaply. It is a weak bet if you stop at the certificate. Trades with local paying customers, such as electrical, cooling, auto and plumbing, can grow into a business." },
  { q: "Can I join ITI after 8th?", a: "Yes, for a smaller set of trades. One government ITI lists Plumber and Sewing Technology as one-year trades for Class 8 pass. Many other trades ask for Class 10, so ask for your state's list." },
  { q: "Which ITI courses can I do after failing Class 10?", a: "Only trades that accept Class 8 pass, if you hold that. Otherwise clear Class 10 through a supplementary attempt or open schooling, then choose a trade. Rules differ by state, so ask the ITI for the written eligibility list." },
  { q: "Which ITI courses are good for commerce students?", a: "ITI eligibility looks at your Class 10 subjects, not your Class 12 stream. COPA and service trades such as Food Production list Class 10 pass. Engineering trades ask for Science and Maths at Class 10. COPA is crowded, so add accounting software or a design skill." },
  { q: "What is the difference between NCVT and SCVT?", a: "National council trades are run under the Directorate General of Training and lead to the National Trade Certificate. State council trades lead to a State Trade Certificate. Since 2018 the national regulator is NCVET, which subsumed the old NCVT. Check which certificate your target job notice accepts." },
  { q: "How do I check if a private ITI is genuine?", a: "Search for it on the NCVT MIS portal by state and district, then confirm the specific trade is affiliated for the session you are joining. Visit the workshop, ask for a written fee list and last batch's placement list, and pay only against receipts." },
  { q: "What is the apprenticeship stipend after ITI?", a: "Public sector notices in 2026 list Rs 10,560 a month for one-year trades and Rs 11,040 for two-year trades at HAL and Nuclear Fuel Complex, and Rs 10,900 for diploma and Rs 12,300 for graduate apprentices. Employers can pay differently, so read each notice." },
  { q: "Can an ITI holder get a government job?", a: "Yes. Railway Group D accepts a Class 10 pass or an NCVT or SCVT ITI at Level 1, and PSU and defence units list ITI apprentices. Competition is high and there are several stages, so keep a trade skill running in parallel." },
  { q: "Can I do a diploma after ITI?", a: "Often through second-year entry, where a Class 10 plus two-year ITI in an appropriate trade qualifies. One polytechnic notice also offers bridge classes in Maths, Physics and Drawing. Most states use an entrance test and the matching branch, and direct admission is rare." },
  { q: "ITI or polytechnic: which is better?", a: "ITI is shorter, cheaper and workshop-led. A polytechnic diploma has more theory and a cleaner path to lateral entry in B.Tech. Choose by the work you can do, the employers near you and how soon you must earn." },
  { q: "How much does an ITI electrician earn?", a: "We found no reliable national figure, so we give none. Check current job listings and ask working electricians. Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles, not exact or guaranteed amounts." },
  { q: "Will electric vehicles and AI hurt ITI jobs?", a: "Some routine tasks will shrink, but electric vehicles, solar and automation also create service work. Learn the trade plus one digital layer, and check your local employers before choosing a new-age trade." },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    "@type": "Question",
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
  })),
};
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para" id="answer">
    <strong>ITI career options are strongest when you pick a trade with a paying local customer, take an apprenticeship, and add one skill beside the certificate.</strong>
    Electrical, cooling, auto and plumbing trades can grow into your own service business. The decisions that unlock high income opportunities are made at 15 or 16, so check the trade, the employers near you and the ITI's affiliation before you pay.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Rank trades by who pays you directly. Trades you can sell as a service lead toward a high-value skill portfolio and earlier financial freedom. Desk-type trades and new-age trades are bets.</li>
      <li>Entry is Class 8 for a few trades and Class 10 for most. Engineering trades ask for Science and Maths at Class 10, and your Class 12 stream does not matter.</li>
      <li>Check any private ITI on the NCVT MIS portal, by trade, before paying. A false affiliation can cost you the certificate that recruiters accept.</li>
      <li>Next step: pass the Bench, Boss and Business Check on two trades, then apply for a one-year apprenticeship in your trade.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    Browse more diploma and trade decisions in <a href="/blog/college-degrees/">college and degree guides</a>. If you are still comparing every route open after Class 10, start with <a href="/blog/stream-selection/career-options-after-10th/">career options after 10th</a>.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="basics">What ITI means: NCVT, SCVT and the certificate</h2>

  <p>
    An Industrial Training Institute teaches a trade through mostly workshop practice. Government and private ITIs teach the same national syllabus when they are affiliated for that trade.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">National route</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">NCVT trades, National Trade Certificate</div>
      <p>The Directorate General of Training runs the Craftsmen Training Scheme and conducts the All India Trade Test. Pass it and you receive the National Trade Certificate. Since 2018 the regulator is NCVET, which took over from the old council.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">State route</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">SCVT trades, State Trade Certificate</div>
      <p>State councils run trades that are not on the national list, and issue a State Trade Certificate. Some trades appear in both lists with different entry rules, such as Diesel Mechanic in one state.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Size and shape</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Over 130 specialties, 169 trades by one count</div>
      <p>Sources count differently: one overview says over 130 specialties, while a skills non-profit counts 169 trades and roughly 11,700 private and 3,300 government ITIs. Treat both as rough.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Hours</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">70:30 practical to theory</div>
      <p>The revised scheme described by the same non-profit has 1,200 learning hours a year plus 150 hours of on-the-job training with a nearby employer, and a 120-hour employability module that includes English and digital basics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="eligibility">ITI eligibility: after 8th, 10th, 10th fail and 12th</h2>

  <p>
    Rules vary by state and trade, so use this table to know what to ask, not as the final word.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where you stand</th>
          <th>What usually opens</th>
          <th>Watch out</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {eligibilityRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Where you stand"><strong>{item.level}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What usually opens">{item.opens}</td>
            <td data-label="Watch out">{item.watch}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: a state ITI admission page listing trade-wise length and eligibility, a Wikipedia overview of ITIs, a state apprenticeship listing, and national skill institute pages. Admissions usually run yearly, with sessions starting in August, and many government ITIs fill seats by merit or a written test while private ITIs admit directly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trades">ITI trades by sector: the real daily work and who hires</h2>

  <p>
    A trade name tells you little. This table groups trades by sector and gives the entry and length shown in the sources we could read.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Sector</th>
          <th>Trades</th>
          <th>Entry and length found</th>
          <th>Who hires</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {tradeRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Sector"><strong>{item.sector}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Trades">{item.trades}</td>
            <td data-label="Entry and length found">{item.entry}</td>
            <td data-label="Who hires">{item.hires}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Lengths and entry differ by state, institute and year. Always ask the ITI for the current prospectus for your exact trade.
  </p>

  <h2 id="scale">Trades ranked by scalability and the sales layer</h2>

  <p>
    We lead with trades where customers pay you directly. No ITI trade is rated fully scalable on its own, because a certificate alone has a fixed pay ladder. Crowded and narrow options stay in, marked with their risk.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Trade</th>
          <th>Our rating</th>
          <th>Why</th>
          <th>Who pays, and the first sales step</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {scaleRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Trade"><strong>{item.trade}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Our rating"><span class={item.cls}>{item.label}</span></td>
            <td data-label="Why">{item.why}</td>
            <td data-label="Who pays, and the first sales step">{item.who}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ratings follow our scalability rule. Scalable with a business layer means you must plan the layer: a service, a workshop or a contracting line. The sales steps are basic starting points and promise no income.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path to notice:</strong> an electrician, cooling or vehicle mechanic who builds a base of repeat customers can set the price, add customers and later hire helpers. That is why local service trades lead our table. It needs tools, any licence your state asks for, and a check of the official GST and Udyam portals before you scale.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="check">The Bench, Boss and Business Check</h2>

  <p>
    Use this on every trade you shortlist. It takes a few conversations, not a few months.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {benchChecks.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="checkpoint__body">
          <div class="checkpoint__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <div class="checkpoint__note">{item.note}</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    A trade is only the core skill. A high-value skill portfolio adds a multiplier such as estimating, a digital tool or English for customers, and visible proof such as a job log. That whole set, not the certificate, moves you toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <p>
    Plain English with customers, a few contacts among contractors and dealers, and a phone number people remember do more for a tradesperson than a second certificate. Almost every ITI trade is on-site work, so only COPA and drawing work can be done remotely from a lower-cost city. A trade is not a lesser path: it becomes weak only when you stop learning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fake">Fake ITI affiliation: the six-step check before you pay</h2>

  <p>
    Private ITIs now outnumber government ones by the non-profit's count, and many are genuine. Some claim an affiliation they do not hold. A false claim can leave you with a paper that recruiters reject.
  </p>

  <div class="mistakes-list">
    {affiliationSteps.map((item) => (
      <div class="mistake-item">
        <div class="mistake-item__num">{item.num}</div>
        <div class="mistake-item__content">
          <div class="mistake-item__title">{item.title}</div>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      A bright brochure, a placement promise or a "government certificate" on a banner proves nothing. Only the portal entry for your trade and session does. If the portal and the institute disagree, trust the portal and walk away.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="apprentice">Apprenticeship after ITI: NAPS and NATS</h2>

  <p>
    Apprenticeship India has two tracks: NAPS for trade-qualified candidates and NATS for graduate and diploma technician apprentices. As an ITI pass-out you usually use NAPS.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>What to check</th>
          <th>What 2026 notices show</th>
          <th>The catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What to check"><strong>Stipend</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What 2026 notices show">HAL lists Rs 10,560 a month for one-year ITI trades and Rs 11,040 for trades of two years or more, Rs 10,900 for diploma and Rs 12,300 for graduate apprentices. Nuclear Fuel Complex lists Rs 11,040 for trades such as Electrician, Fitter and COPA and Rs 10,560 for Welder, Plumber, Carpenter and Diesel Mechanic.</td>
          <td data-label="The catch">A stipend is not a salary. Read the notice, because the figure depends on the employer and trade.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What to check"><strong>How to apply</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What 2026 notices show">Register on the Apprenticeship India portal, complete the profile with e-KYC, and apply to the named establishment. The trade on your ITI certificate must match the trade you choose.</td>
          <td data-label="The catch">You can do an apprenticeship once in the same trade, and notices exclude people who already completed one.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="What to check"><strong>Length and fee</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What 2026 notices show">One year under the Apprentices Act, 1961. Neither notice listed an application fee.</td>
          <td data-label="The catch">It does not guarantee a regular job. Many units offer interview-based shortlisting, so seats are the first competitive stage.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The apprenticeship is where employer-only becomes real. The employer pays you and decides whether to keep you. Treat it as paid proof: keep a job log, ask for a written reference, and learn how the shop quotes and bills jobs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="govt">Government and PSU routes for ITI holders</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>What the notices say</th>
          <th>The catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {govtRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What the notices say">{item.notice}</td>
            <td data-label="The catch">{item.catch}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Sources: recruitment news summaries for Railway Group D and PSU apprenticeship notices (2026), and search summaries of Army notices. We could not open the official Army and Railway sites, so check the current notice before you plan.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Two stages of competition</p>
    <p>
      Government and PSU routes have a hard entry stage and often a second hard stage, such as a physical test or the move from apprentice to regular job. These are employer-only routes, so the sales layer matters for your side service or your next move. Keep a trade skill that earns even if the exam does not work out.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="diploma">ITI to diploma: second-year entry and its limits</h2>

  <p>
    A Class 10 pass with a two-year ITI in an appropriate trade can qualify for second-year diploma entry in some places. One polytechnic notice states exactly that and adds bridge classes in Maths, Physics and Drawing.
  </p>

  <div class="work-style-grid">
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Entry</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Usually a state test, same branch</div>
      <p>Education forums and notices describe a state technical education test and a diploma branch that matches your ITI trade. Direct admission on ITI marks is rare.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Time math</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">About four years to a diploma</div>
      <p>Two years of ITI plus the last two years of the diploma is about four years, against three years for a diploma taken straight after Class 10. The extra year buys a trade certificate and workshop skill.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="work-style-card">
      <div class="work-style-card__label">Next bridge</div>
      <div class="work-style-card__title">Diploma then B.Tech</div>
      <p>A diploma can then lead to lateral entry in B.Tech. The rules for that step are in our guide to the <a href="/blog/college-degrees/polytechnic-diploma-after-10th-which-trade-to-choose/">polytechnic diploma after 10th</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A forum page dated August 2026 claims ITI holders can now join degrees directly. We could not confirm it on an official source, so do not plan on it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vs">ITI or polytechnic: a short comparison</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Question</th>
          <th>ITI</th>
          <th>Polytechnic diploma</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {vsRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
